/*
Detection: SSH Brute Force
MITRE ATT&CK: T1110 - Brute Force
Data source: Linux auth.log (Metasploitable / any Linux endpoint)
Logic: alert when a single source IP produces many failed SSH logons
       in a short window, optionally followed by a success.

This is a TEMPLATE. Once Splunk is ingesting auth.log, run this search,
confirm it fires against your Hydra attack, then save it as an alert and
record the investigation in ../investigations/.
*/

index=linux sourcetype=linux_secure "Failed password"
| rex "from (?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| bin _time span=5m
| stats count AS failed_attempts values(user) AS targeted_users by _time, src_ip
| where failed_attempts > 10
| sort - failed_attempts

/* Tuning notes:
   - Threshold (10 / 5 min) is a starting point: tune to your lab's noise.
   - A true positive: many failures from one IP, then a 'Accepted password'.
   - A false positive: a user fat-fingering their password a few times.
   - Escalate when failures are followed by a success from the same IP.
*/
